.SH "BUGS"
\fBreccord\-delete\-database\.py\fR is a typo (double \fBc\fR) and will fail with a command\-not\-found error, silently skipping the database deletion unless \fBset \-e\fR is active\. The correct name is likely \fBrecord\-delete\-database\.py\fR\.
.P
The script does not use \fBset \-e\fR, so if \fBrecord\-stop\.sh\fR or \fBrecord\-clean\.sh\fR fails, execution continues to the database deletion and mrproper steps regardless\.
.P
\fBexit \-1\fR is used for the confirmation refusal exit\. POSIX does not guarantee the behaviour of negative exit codes; most shells interpret \fB\-1\fR as 255, but this is not portable\.
.P
The warning message is printed with \fBecho\fR rather than directed to standard error, so it will be swallowed if stdout is redirected\.
.P
\fBlib\.record\.mrproper\.sh\fR is sourced unconditionally as the final step but there is no check that it exists; if the file is absent, the source command will fail with an error, making the success of an otherwise complete reset appear as a failure\.
